Elementary exclusion of order divisible by21 in O7(3)
-----------------------------------------------------

The simple group convention O7(3)=P Omega_7(3) here is the group of order
4585351680. It has a faithful seven-dimensional representation over F3;
this is explicitly listed by the primary Online ATLAS O7(3) page. The
argument only uses an embedding in GL7(F3), so no detailed orthogonal
centralizer formula is required.

Let x be an order7 matrix in GL7(F3). The multiplicative order of 3 modulo7
is6: the successive residues are3,2,6,4,5,1. Since characteristic3 does
not divide7, x is semisimple. The polynomial Phi_7 is irreducible over F3,
so a nontrivial irreducible constituent for x has dimension6. In dimension7,
x therefore has exactly one such constituent and one fixed line. The two
constituents are nonisomorphic. Its full linear centralizer is consequently

                    F_(3^6)^* x F_3^*,

of order (3^6-1)(3-1)=1456=2^4*7*13, which is prime to3.

If a group element g has order divisible by21, its finite cyclic subgroup
contains an element u of exact order21. Then x=u^3 has order7 and y=u^7
has order3, and y centralizes x. In a faithful GL7(F3) representation this
contradicts the centralizer order above. Therefore O7(3) has no element
whose order is divisible by21.

There is no index-two-extension loophole: an order21 element in O7(3):2
maps trivially to its quotient of order2, so it would already lie in O7(3).
This observation does NOT permit replacing the exact simple maximal types
with arbitrary covering groups. In particular, the central triple cover
3.O7(3) has an additional central3 and is a different subject; it cannot
be substituted for either retained O7(3) maximal subgroup.

Maximal-cover deduction
-----------------------

Suppose H were proper in G. It lies in a maximal subgroup M. The first
witness makes13 divide |M|, so the imported cover restricts M to the two
O7(3) classes or the Tits type. An element of order divisible by21 cannot
lie in either O7(3) class by the preceding proof. It cannot lie in the Tits
group because7 does not divide that group's order. All possible M are
excluded. Thus H=G.

